    title: "เป็น, คือ, อยู่: is, and is at",
    blurb: "Say what someone is, what something is, and where it is, and know when Thai needs no word for is at all.",
    deck: "About me",
    body: `
<p>English uses one verb, "to be", for very different jobs: "I am a teacher", "This is Anna", "The bathroom is over there", "The food is good". Thai splits these jobs, and for one of them it uses no verb at all.</p>
<h3>เป็น: jobs, roles and nationality</h3>
<p>Use <b>เป็น</b> <i>bpen</i> to say what someone is: a job, a role, a nationality. There is no word for "a".</p>
{{ex:ผมเป็นครู|phǒm bpen khruu|I'm a teacher. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:ฉันเป็นคนอเมริกัน|chǎn bpen khon à-mee-rí-gan|I'm American. (female speaker)}}
{{ex:เพื่อนผมเป็นครู|phûuean phǒm bpen khruu|My friend is a teacher. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:พี่สาวฉันเป็นพยาบาล|phîi-sǎao chǎn bpen phá-yaa-baan|My older sister is a nurse. (female speaker)}}
<h3>คือ: pointing out and defining</h3>
<p><b>คือ</b> <i>khuue</i> links two things that are the same: "this is…", "the answer is…". You hear it most when introducing someone with <b>นี่</b> <i>nîi</i> (this).</p>
{{ex:นี่คือเพื่อนฉัน|nîi khuue phûuean chǎn|This is my friend. (female speaker)}}
<h3>อยู่: where something is</h3>
<p>For place, Thai uses <b>อยู่</b> <i>yùu</i>, "to be at, to stay, to live". เป็น never means "is at".</p>
{{ex:ห้องน้ำอยู่ที่ไหน|hâwng-náam yùu thîi-nǎi|Where is the bathroom?}}
{{ex:ผมอยู่ที่บ้าน|phǒm yùu thîi bâan|I'm at home. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:ตลาดอยู่ใกล้บ้าน|dtà-làat yùu glâi bâan|The market is near the house.}}
{{ex:บ้านผมอยู่ไกล|bâan phǒm yùu glai|My house is far. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:ธนาคารอยู่ไกล|thá-naa-khaan yùu glai|The bank is far.}}
<p>Listen for ใกล้ and ไกล here: near has a falling tone, far a mid tone. In a sentence about place the two sound very close, so say the tone clearly and, if a driver looks unsure, point.</p>
<h3>No verb before describing words</h3>
<p>Thai describing words, such as hungry, delicious or far, work like verbs on their own. "The food is delicious" is just "food delicious". The same goes for feelings: tired, happy and full need no verb either. Adding เป็น here is the classic English-speaker mistake, and Thais will understand you but hear it as odd.</p>
{{ex:อาหารอร่อย|aa-hǎan à-ràwi|The food is delicious.}}
{{ex:ฉันหิวค่ะ|chǎn hǐu khâ|I'm hungry. (female speaker)}}
<h3>Not a teacher: ไม่ใช่</h3>
<p>To say someone is not something, use <b>ไม่ใช่</b> <i>mâi châi</i> before the noun, not ไม่เป็น. ไม่ได้เป็น is also correct.</p>
{{ex:ผมไม่ใช่ครู|phǒm mâi châi khruu|I'm not a teacher. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:ผมไม่ได้เป็นครู|phǒm mâi dâai bpen khruu|I'm not a teacher. (male speaker)}}
<p><b>ใช่</b> <i>châi</i> on its own means "that's right"; it answers a question about what something is.</p>
{{ex:คุณเป็นคนอเมริกันไหม|khun bpen khon à-mee-rí-gan mǎi|Are you American?}}
{{ex:ใช่ครับ|châi khráp|Yes, I am. (male speaker)}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> เป็น before a describing word or a place. ผมเป็นหิว → ผมหิว, I'm hungry; ผมเป็นที่บ้าน → ผมอยู่ที่บ้าน, I'm at home.</div>
`,
    check: [
      ["How do you say I'm hungry (male speaker)?",["ผมเป็นหิว","ผมหิว","ผมอยู่หิว","ผมคือหิว"],1,"Describing words need no เป็น: ผมหิว."],
      ["Which word says where something is?",["อยู่","เป็น","คือ","ใช่"],0,"อยู่ is for place: ห้องน้ำอยู่ที่ไหน."],
      ["How do you say I'm not a teacher (female speaker)?",["ฉันไม่เป็นครู","ฉันเป็นไม่ครู","ไม่ฉันครู","ฉันไม่ใช่ครู"],3,"Before a noun, not is ไม่ใช่ (or ไม่ได้เป็น), never ไม่เป็น."]
    ],
